WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.660 --> 00:00:05.600
The very last scene of The Last Jedi garnered
gasps from packed theaters across the globe.

00:00:05.710 --> 00:00:09.620
Yep: that little boy used the Force to guide
a broom into his hand before pausing from

00:00:09.620 --> 00:00:12.940
his work to stare into space, daydreaming
about the Resistance.

00:00:13.080 --> 00:00:16.160
Who is that little boy and what exactly did
that mean?

00:00:16.360 --> 00:00:20.120
And more importantly, what does the scene
mean for the future of Star Wars?

00:00:20.340 --> 00:00:24.359
Let's break down what could potentially be
the most important scene in The Last Jedi—and

00:00:24.360 --> 00:00:27.100
be forewarned, there are more spoilers ahead.

00:00:27.800 --> 00:00:28.600
He's a slave

00:00:29.180 --> 00:00:34.000
To provide a little refresher, one of The
Last Jedi's plots sees Finn and Rose traveling

00:00:34.000 --> 00:00:37.380
to the planet Cantonica to find a hacker in
the city of Canto Bight.

00:00:37.520 --> 00:00:42.060
The casino city—more Monaco than Las Vegas—is
the ultimate hangout for the wealthy.

00:00:42.280 --> 00:00:43.860
But that doesn't tell the whole story.

00:00:43.940 --> 00:00:49.000
As Rose points out, drawing on her own painful
past, the people working in Canto Bight are

00:00:49.000 --> 00:00:49.500
slaves.

00:00:49.520 --> 00:00:53.660
It's during this sequence that we first encounter
the boy in one of the stables as Finn and

00:00:53.679 --> 00:00:57.280
Rose escape—he's among the child slaves
that clean and sleep in the stables while

00:00:57.280 --> 00:01:01.400
caring for the Fathiers, the space ponies
who end up serving as Finn and Rose's way

00:01:01.400 --> 00:01:01.900
out.

00:01:02.340 --> 00:01:03.240
We know his name

00:01:03.820 --> 00:01:08.320
The Star Wars: The Last Jedi Visual Dictionary
tells us that the boy is named Temiri Blagg,

00:01:08.440 --> 00:01:12.440
and according to IMDb, first-timer Temirlan
Blaev portrays the boy.

00:01:12.560 --> 00:01:16.860
Clearly, Blagg is at the least Force sensitive,
and that's nothing to scoff at in the Star

00:01:16.860 --> 00:01:17.860
Wars universe.

00:01:17.900 --> 00:01:21.800
In fact, over the course of the current trilogy,
he's one of only a handful of people we've

00:01:21.810 --> 00:01:27.190
seen use the Force—a list that includes
heavy hitters like Luke, Rey, Leia, Kylo Ren,

00:01:27.190 --> 00:01:28.280
Snoke, and Yoda.

00:01:28.580 --> 00:01:30.460
In other words, he's probably a big deal.

00:01:31.340 --> 00:01:32.120
He's a nobody

00:01:32.480 --> 00:01:36.960
Aside from offering up some of the coolest
space battles and most exciting action sequences

00:01:36.960 --> 00:01:41.890
in the franchise, The Last Jedi put a lot
of effort into shaking up the Star Wars mythology—which

00:01:41.890 --> 00:01:45.460
is another way of saying that a lot of what
we thought we knew about the saga just went

00:01:45.460 --> 00:01:47.250
out like trash from a Star Destroyer.

00:01:47.300 --> 00:01:52.360
Rey, the powerful Force-sensitive hero of
the new trilogy, turns out not to be the secret

00:01:52.360 --> 00:01:54.480
offspring of one of the story's heavy hitters.

00:01:54.620 --> 00:01:57.300
In fact, her parents were basically space
trash.

00:01:57.360 --> 00:02:01.860
In the new Star Wars universe, you don't have
to be a Skywalker or a Kenobi to be important.

00:02:02.140 --> 00:02:06.329
Along those lines, Temiri Blagg doesn't appear
to be related to anyone you've heard of; his

00:02:06.329 --> 00:02:09.100
parents abandoned him, just as Rey's parents
left her.

00:02:09.280 --> 00:02:13.840
As the Skywalker saga comes to a close, we're
learning that anyone can be Force-sensitive,

00:02:14.060 --> 00:02:17.620
from a daughter of drunkards to a slave boy
who sweeps up Fathier scat.

00:02:18.340 --> 00:02:19.520
He won't be a Jedi

00:02:20.020 --> 00:02:24.340
Kylo Ren spelled out the new plan for the
movies going forward—no more Jedi, no more

00:02:24.340 --> 00:02:24.840
Sith.

00:02:25.060 --> 00:02:29.880
Temiri Blagg is Force sensitive, and he might
do something with it, but he won't be a Jedi,

00:02:29.880 --> 00:02:32.120
because the order as it existed is gone.

00:02:32.420 --> 00:02:37.379
Rey isn't one, at least as it's been traditionally
defined, so as far as we know there aren't

00:02:37.380 --> 00:02:38.920
any Jedi left to teach him.

00:02:39.060 --> 00:02:44.080
And yes, the Jedi sacred books survived—stowed
away by Rey on the Millennium Falcon—but

00:02:44.080 --> 00:02:48.560
studying from a book is very different from
the master/padawan dynamic built up over generations

00:02:48.560 --> 00:02:49.200
of Jedi.

00:02:50.000 --> 00:02:50.940
He could be a New Hope

00:02:51.620 --> 00:02:56.120
As Luke vowed before becoming one with the
Force in the final act, he won't be the last

00:02:56.129 --> 00:03:00.280
Jedi—but what it means to be one, and who
gets to carry that mantle going forward, is

00:03:00.280 --> 00:03:00.820
wide open.

00:03:00.900 --> 00:03:05.860
It likely wasn't a coincidence that The Last
Jedi ended with Blagg staring off into a star

00:03:05.860 --> 00:03:10.000
cluster, uncertain of his future, just as
Luke did at the end of The Empire Strikes

00:03:10.000 --> 00:03:10.500
Back.

00:03:10.520 --> 00:03:14.360
You could make the case that Blagg is the
new Luke—without the pedigree, and without

00:03:14.360 --> 00:03:16.160
the promise of proper Jedi training.

00:03:17.000 --> 00:03:18.000
Or maybe he isn't?

00:03:18.360 --> 00:03:22.860
Then again, the odds of Temiri Blagg being
the only other Force-sensitive character besides

00:03:22.870 --> 00:03:27.540
Rey in that galaxy far, far away is pretty
slim, and that also plays into the new Star

00:03:27.540 --> 00:03:28.320
Wars canon.

00:03:28.560 --> 00:03:31.160
Blagg won't be the only one—there will be
others.

00:03:31.320 --> 00:03:33.020
Maybe he never makes it off Cantonica?

00:03:33.100 --> 00:03:35.120
Maybe he doesn't pop up in Episode 9?

00:03:35.360 --> 00:03:39.740
Both of those scenarios would fit into what
Star Wars is now: a brand new series, where

00:03:39.740 --> 00:03:43.240
anyone can use the Force, and the Sith and
Jedi as we knew them are no more.

00:03:43.820 --> 00:03:44.819
Thanks for watching!

00:03:44.819 --> 00:03:47.140
Click the Looper icon to subscribe to our
YouTube channel.

00:03:47.140 --> 00:03:49.240
Plus check out all this cool stuff we know
you'll love, too!

